---
import BaseLayout from "../../../shared/components/BaseLayout.astro";
import { MAIN_SITE_URL } from "../../../shared/config/config";

const siteTitle = "Porn Recovery Knowledge Platform";
const jsonLd = {
  "@context": "https://schema.org",
  "@type": "CollectionPage",
  name: "About Relapse",
  url: MAIN_SITE_URL + "/relapse/",
  description: "What relapse is, why it is common, and how people describe recovering after one.",
};
---
<BaseLayout
  siteTitle={siteTitle}
  siteUrl={MAIN_SITE_URL}
  title="About Relapse — Porn Recovery Knowledge Platform"
  description="Relapse is common and is not failure. What community members report about relapse, and how they describe recovering afterward — without shame and without causal claims."
  canonical="/relapse/"
  jsonLd={jsonLd}
  breadcrumbs={[{ name: "Home", url: "/" }, { name: "About Relapse", url: "/relapse/" }]}
>
  <h1>About Relapse</h1>
  <p>A relapse means returning to pornography use after a period of stopping or reducing it.
  Community members overwhelmingly describe relapse as a common part of the process — not
  as failure.</p>

  <h2>What people report about relapse</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>It is common.</strong> Many members describe several attempts and setbacks
    before longer periods of control.</li>
    <li><strong>It is usually tied to a trigger.</strong> Stress, boredom, being alone, and
    late-night phone use are among the situations people name before a relapse.</li>
    <li><strong>The shame can be harder than the slip itself.</strong> Members frequently
    describe self-criticism as the part that made restarting difficult.</li>
  </ul>
  <p class="note">These are community observations, not established facts about relapse.
  See <a href="/methodology/">Methodology</a> for how observations are handled.</p>

  <h2>Why this platform avoids shame language</h2>
  <p>You will not find phrases like "just try harder" or "real men don't relapse" here.
  Those framings are not supported by evidence and are reported by many members to make
  recovery harder. Instead, this platform treats a relapse as information: a chance to
  learn what preceded it and what to change next.</p>

  <h2>Where to go next</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><a href="/relapse/triggers/">Common relapse triggers</a></li>
    <li><a href="/relapse/recovery-after-relapse/">Recovering after a relapse</a></li>
    <li><a href="/relapse/after-7-days/">Relapse after 7 days</a></li>
    <li><a href="/relapse/after-30-days/">Relapse after 30 days</a></li>
    <li><a href="/relapse/after-90-days/">Relapse after 90 days</a></li>
    <li><a href="/problems/what-counts-as-a-relapse/">What counts as a relapse?</a></li>
    <li><a href="/problems/how-do-i-handle-a-relapse-without-shame/">Handling relapse without shame</a></li>
  </ul>
</BaseLayout>
